WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.279
Diego Alvarez: Morning, Amanda. How's the Austin office today?

2
00:00:09.641 --> 00:00:12.193
Amanda Pierce: Busy. Quarter close stuff. How about you?

3
00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:21.369
Diego Alvarez: Same, same. Hey, before we get into it, I saw your call volume ticked up last month. That still holding?

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:32.284
Amanda Pierce: Yeah, we're around 38,000 calls a month across the three states now. Texas is carrying most of it. So, Phoenix is growing faster than we planned, honestly.

5
00:00:33.745 --> 00:00:41.903
Amanda Pierce: And we just added two bilingual reps on the Texas floor, so that's been an adjustment. You know, Training, scheduling, all of it.

6
00:00:42.473 --> 00:00:48.767
Diego Alvarez: You know, Good problem. Are the new reps on the same call flow or did you split them out?

7
00:00:49.374 --> 00:00:53.717
Amanda Pierce: Same flow for now. We'll probably you know, revisit in Q4.

8
00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:58.251
Diego Alvarez: Got it. So what's on your list today?

9
00:00:59.118 --> 00:01:08.547
Amanda Pierce: A couple things. The first one is, um, I need to flag something for renewal planning. Finance and procurement are pushing back on how we're billed.

10
00:01:08.547 --> 00:01:13.261
Amanda Pierce: The add-ons are priced separately from the base, and they want one number.

11
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.867
Diego Alvarez: Which add-ons are they looking at?

12
00:01:18.179 --> 00:01:32.549
Amanda Pierce: All of them, really. But the one they keep coming back to is Signal AI. From their side, it looks like we're paying for call tracking and then paying again for the signals on top of the same calls.

13
00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:35.497
Amanda Pierce: I've explained it, but the optics are hard.

14
00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:40.346
Diego Alvarez: Okay. Is this a procurement thing or is it coming from your team?

15
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:48.382
Amanda Pierce: It's It's procurement. They've got a new VP over there, and and she's going line by um, line on every vendor.

16
00:01:48.382 --> 00:01:51.276
Amanda Pierce: I don't think it's personal, but it's real.

17
00:01:51.547 --> 00:01:56.275
Diego Alvarez: Makes sense. Can I ask what a good outcome looks like from your seat?

18
00:01:56.596 --> 00:02:08.703
Amanda Pierce: Honestly, one line item. Or at least a clearer story about why it's structured this way. I I can defend a number, I just can't defend the shape of the bill right now.

19
00:02:09.798 --> 00:02:19.094
Diego Alvarez: Okay, that's helpful. Let me take that back and see what's possible on the packaging side. I'm not going to promise anything on the call.

20
00:02:19.558 --> 00:02:20.358
Amanda Pierce: Fair.

21
00:02:20.786 --> 00:02:21.586
Diego Alvarez: What else?

22
00:02:23.003 --> 00:02:33.666
Amanda Pierce: The The other one is a tracking issue. Our Our web team rebuilt the quote form last month, and on the new page the number stops swapping after the first load.

23
00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:42.265
Amanda Pierce: So if someone lands on the homepage and then clicks through to the quote flow, they get the generic number instead of the campaign one.

24
00:02:43.581 --> 00:02:45.721
Diego Alvarez: When did you first notice?

25
00:02:46.789 --> 00:02:57.364
Amanda Pierce: About three weeks ago. Our marketing ops guy caught it because a paid search campaign was showing zero calls. It's not zero, it's just all landing on the the default number.

26
00:02:58.055 --> 00:03:00.720
Diego Alvarez: Is it every route change or specific ones?

27
00:03:01.258 --> 00:03:08.375
Amanda Pierce: Like, the quote flow is the big one. There might be others, I don't have the full list.

28
00:03:09.365 --> 00:03:21.106
Diego Alvarez: Okay. I'll get our tag team on it, they'll probably want to look at how the page is loading. Can you you send me the URL and the campaign ID?

29
00:03:22.107 --> 00:03:24.847
Amanda Pierce: Yeah, uh, I'll send it this afternoon.

30
00:03:25.966 --> 00:03:26.766
Diego Alvarez: Perfect.

31
00:03:27.787 --> 00:03:38.847
Amanda Pierce: And, um, I'll be straight with you on the renewal. With procurement digging in and this tracking thing open, we've started taking calls from other vendors.

32
00:03:38.847 --> 00:03:43.952
Amanda Pierce: I'm not saying we're leaving, but the timing is what it is.

33
00:03:45.355 --> 00:03:49.318
Diego Alvarez: Like, I appreciate you telling me. When does procurement want an answer?

34
00:03:49.788 --> 00:03:58.302
Amanda Pierce: They'd like a recommendation by early November. Our renewal isn't until the end of next November, but they want the decision locked early this cycle.

35
00:03:59.608 --> 00:04:08.085
Diego Alvarez: Okay. I mean, Let me put together what I can. I'll follow up by Friday with next steps on both.

36
00:04:08.511 --> 00:04:10.061
Amanda Pierce: That works. Thanks, Diego.

37
00:04:10.659 --> 00:04:16.838
Diego Alvarez: Of course. And hey, good luck with the new reps. Bilingual floor is not easy to train.

38
00:04:17.718 --> 00:04:20.030
Amanda Pierce: Tell me about it. Talk Friday.
